# Binaries
/weft
*.exe
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# Test and profiling output
*.test
*.out
coverage.*

# Go workspace files
go.work
go.work.sum

# Build output of the release and docs pipelines. Both are regenerated from
# tracked inputs (.goreleaser.yaml, site/), so neither belongs in the history.
/dist/
/site/public/
/site/resources/
/site/.hugo_build.lock

# Milestone 4 evaluation data: downloaded corpora, the Semantic Scholar response
# cache, committed segments and generated run reports. Gigabytes, and all
# reproducible from docs/EVAL.md. The reference goldens under
# internal/eval/testdata/ are deliberately NOT here — those are the evidence.
/.eval-data/

# weftd's default -data directory. One subdirectory per index, holding committed
# segments and the _source store — the server's working state, reproducible by
# re-indexing and never something to carry in the history. Same reasoning as
# /.eval-data/ above, at a smaller scale.
/.weftd-data/

# Per-milestone TDD plans. Working notes written before the tests, not the
# evidence — that lives in the _test.go files and docs/FINDINGS.md.
docs/testing/
docs/tdd/

# Local tooling, editor, and OS noise
.claude/
.ruff_cache/
__pycache__/
.DS_Store
*.swp
.idea/
.vscode/
.changelog.expected

# The bleve comparison index. Multi-gigabyte, rebuildable with `cd bench && go run . -build`.
/bench/.bleve-index/

# And the comparison binary itself. `go build` in bench/ writes a 20 MiB executable
# named after the directory; README.md there says `go run .`, so nothing needs it
# committed and a platform-specific binary in the history is 20 MiB nobody can use.
/bench/bench
